@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the two paths as a history of humanity
#
# One node, in its own file because it concerns neither the affect scales nor
# any tradition. It belongs to this directory because no transmission carries
# it: it begins in a datable work by a named author and is afterwards reported
# as the old meaning of the two paths.
#

pmc:TwoPathsOfKnowledge a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "The branching of humanity into a right-hand and a left-hand path of knowledge"@en, "Die Verzweigung der Menschheit in einen rechten und einen linken Pfad des Wissens"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that humanity divided at a datable point of its history into two opposed paths of knowledge, a right-hand and a left-hand one, and that the two magics called white and black were sown as seeds at that moment."@en ;
    skos:note "What is new here is not the pair of names, which stands in the orientalist literature from 1832 and is held as pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature. What is new is that the pair becomes a history: two parties, a moment of origin, and a lineage of adepts on either side. Everything the later occult milieu inherits — that there are two camps and that a person may join one of them — rests on this move and not on the naming."@en ;
    skos:note "Checked against the earlier work rather than assumed from it. The full text of Isis Unveiled (1877), both volumes, carries no occurrence of a right-hand or a left-hand path, and none of the tantric vocabulary the names come from. The usual account, which dates the introduction of the terms to 1877, is wrong by eleven years: the passage stands in the work of 1888. See prima-materia#58."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is presented as a quotation from a commentary of unstated antiquity, and it is the commentary rather than the author that later reporting cites. That is what pm:circulatesAs records here, and it is why this node is an origination and not an ordering: an ordering names what it arranges, and this one arranges nothing."@en ;
    pm:originatedBy "Helena P. Blavatsky" ;
    pm:circulatesAs "The ancient meaning of the two paths, credited to a commentary of unstated age rather than to the work of 1888 in which it first stands." ;
    dcterms:date "1888" ;
    dcterms:source "H. P. Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine I: Cosmogenesis (London 1888), in the discussion of the fourth race, at the sentence on the branching of humanity into the right- and left-hand paths of knowledge or Vidya" .

pmc:PolarityHarvestThresholds a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "The two thresholds of polarity and the collapse of the negative one"@en, "Die zwei Schwellen der Polarität und der Zusammenbruch der negativen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a being becomes harvestable on one of two opposed paths at unequal thresholds — a slight majority of service to others on the one, an approach to totality of service to self on the other, with a region of indifference between them — and that the negative path is nevertheless abandoned at the density of unity, because it rests on an omission and what is not cannot endure."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as an origination rather than as the teaching of a tradition. The material was produced between 1981 and 1984 by three named people in a stated procedure, and it presents itself as received rather than composed. That presentation is what pm:circulatesAs records; the node makes no claim about the source of the material, only about where the text begins."@en ;
    skos:note "The two halves of the claim do not sit easily together, and the record keeps them apart rather than choosing. Read from the thresholds alone the system is a dualism with two valid outcomes; read from the argument about the omission it is a monism in which one outcome is provisional. Whoever cites only the thresholds reports a symmetry the same text withdraws. See prima-materia#69."@en ;
    skos:note "One caution for anyone opening the passages. The published wording of the omission argument names the wrong polarity at its first occurrence, and the correction is made in the exchange itself; a reader who quotes the sentence as printed quotes a slip. And the passage about abandoning the negative polarity in the early sixth density stands at 36.12, not at 78.25 as an earlier note in prima-materia#62 had it. 78.25 carries the different statement that the two paths must take in each other."@en ;
    pm:originatedBy "Carla Rueckert as instrument, with Don Elkins as questioner and Jim McCarty as scribe (L/L Research)" ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A cosmology of great antiquity, received from a source outside the authors, rather than a text composed between 1981 and 1984." ;
    dcterms:date "1981/1984" ;
    dcterms:source "The Law of One (The Ra Material), sessions 17.32 and 17.33 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)", "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 36.12 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)", "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 78.25 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)", "The Law of One (The Ra Material), session 85.11 (L/L Research, Louisville 1984)" .
